# Polydentate Ligand Reinforced Chelating to Stabilize Buried Interface toward High‐Performance Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
The instability of the buried interface poses a serious challenge for commercializing perovskite photovoltaic technology. Herein, we report a polydentate ligand reinforced chelating strategy to strengthen the stability of buried interface by managing interfacial defects and stress. The bis(2,2,2-trifluoroethyl) (methoxycarbonylmethyl)phosphonate (BTP) is employed to manipulate the buried interface. The C=O, P=O and two -CF3 functional groups in BTP synergistically passivate the defects from the surface of SnO2 and the bottom surface of the perovskite layer. Moreover, The BTP modification contributes to mitigated interfacial residual tensile stress, promoted perovskite crystallization, and reduced interfacial energy barrier. The multidentate ligand modulation strategy is appropriate for different perovskite compositions. Due to much reduced nonradiative recombination and heightened interface contact, the device with BTP yields a promising power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 24.63 %, which is one of the highest efficiencies ever reported for devices fabricated in the air environment. The unencapsulated BTP-modified devices degrade to 98.6 % and 84.2 % of their initial PCE values after over 3000 h of aging in the ambient environment and after 1728 h of thermal stress, respectively. This work provides insights into strengthening the stability of the buried interface by engineering multidentate chelating ligand molecules.

## Introduction
Metal halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attracted significant attention in the photovoltaic field because of their exceptional performance.[1–6] It has now achieved an unprecedented $26.1\%$ certified record efficiency, which is comparable to that of monocrystalline silicon solar cells.[7] However, the poor long-term stability still poses a serious challenge to the commercialization of perovskite photovoltaic technology.[8] The each of functional layers and interfaces in PSCs is closely associated with its long-term stability. Among them, the buried interface in regular n-i-p configuration plays a critical role in realizing stable and highly efficient PSCs.[9–12]
The $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is employed as an electron transport layer (ETL) in efficient and stable regular PSCs due to its high electron mobility, low-temperature preparation, low hysteresis, poor photocatalytic activity, and matched energy band alignment.[13–15] The oxygen vacancy and uncoordinated $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ defects are usually present in $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ ETL. Simultaneously, various charged defects usually exist at the bottom surface of perovskite films, such as undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , halide vacancy, cation vacancy, $\mathrm{Pb{I}}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}$ anti-site defects, etc. The defects from the surface of ETL and the bottom surface of the perovskite layer would result in severe nonradiative recombination, which can deteriorate the stability of the buried interface and final devices.[16,17] In addition to interfacial defects, the interfacial residual strain is frequently caused by the lattice mismatch and discrepancy in thermal expansion coefficient between the substrate and perovskite, which would lead to nonradiative recombination and reduce the stability of the buried interface.[18–21] Finally, the interfacial contact quality between $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL and the perovskite layer directly impacts the stability of the buried interface. In a word, the stability of the buried interface greatly depends on the interfacial defects, interfacial residual strain and interfacial contact quality.
Interface engineering is one feasible and effective strategy for overcoming the instability of buried interface.[22–24] Among various modifiers, Lewis base ligand molecules have exhibited huge potential in manipulating buried interface.[12,25,26] To strengthen interfacial chemical interaction, multi-active-site ligand molecules have been developed to modulate the interface between perovskite and carrier transport layer, which heightened interfacial defect passivation and stress release, finally enhancing interface stability.[25,27–29] The selection of functional groups is of crucial importance for designing multidentate ligand interface modification molecules. The strong coordination groups $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ have been widely adopted to manage undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ defects at the buried interface.[12,13,25,26,30–32] In addition, the fluorination strategy is one effective approach for strengthening interfacial chemical interactions via enriching chemical bonding modes including hydrogen bonds and coordination bonds.[21,33,34] Taken together, the polydentate ligand molecules with $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ and F groups should be developed to modulate and stable buried interface for achieving stable and efficient PSCs.
In this work, we adopted polydentate ligand bis (2,2,2-trifluoroethyl) (methoxycarbonylmethyl)phosphonate (BTP) to stabilize buried interface by passivating interfacial defects and releasing interfacial stress. It was demonstrated that multiple functional groups in BTP, including $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=O}$ and two $-\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ , can synergistically heal undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ defects along with halide vacancy defects. At the same time, ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ groups can suppress $\mathrm{FA}^{+}$ migration by forming a hydrogen bond. BTP modification also resulted in improved perovskite morphology and energy band alignment. After BTP modulation, interfacial nonradiative recombination was minimized and interfacial contact was reinforced. The different perovskite compositions were used to reveal the universality of BTP modification in boosting device performance. The device with BTP exhibited a promising PCE of $24.63\%$ , which is among the highest efficiency ever reported for the PSCs fabricated in ambient conditions. The BTP-modified unencapsulated devices maintained $98.6\%$ of their initial PCE after over 3000 hours of aging in the ambient environment under a relative humidity (RH) of $10{-}20\%$ , and $84.2\%$ of their original PCE after $1728\mathrm{h}$ of thermal stress at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This work provides a simple and effective method for stabilizing the buried interface by designing a multidentate chelating ligand modifier.
## Results and Discussion
The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) techniques were employed to investigate the interaction mechanisms of BTP with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and perovskite layers. Compared to the Sn 3d deconvolution spectra of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film at 486.75 and $495.16\mathrm{eV}$ , the peaks of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -BTP film at 487.12 and $495.53\mathrm{eV}$ exhibited a shift towards higher binding energy (Figure 1a). This shift suggests that the electron cloud density surrounding Sn atoms has been altered due to the chemical interaction between $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and BTP.[35] Concomitantly, the results were further validated through the analysis of the binding energy change of O 1s (Figure 1b). The $\mathrm{~o~}$ 1s peaks observed at $530.61\mathrm{eV}$ for the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film and ${531.04\mathrm{eV}}$ for the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ - BTP film corresponded to the lattice oxygen $\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{lat}}\right)$ present on the surface of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film. We observed a shift in the $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{v}}$ peaks of BTP films towards higher binding energy by $0.45\mathrm{eV}$ after BTP modification. After the surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ was modified by BTP, the ratio of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{lat}}$ to $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{v}}$ was increased from 1.47 to 2.2, indicating that oxygen vacancy and uncoordinated $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ defects were effectively passivated by polydentate ligand BTP. As illustrated in Figure 1c and d, the peaks of F 1s $(688.48\mathrm{eV})$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~P~}}2\boldsymbol{\mathrm{p}}$ $(133.71\mathrm{eV})$ in pure BTP exhibited a shift of $0.43\mathrm{eV}$ and $5.55\mathrm{eV}$ towards higher binding energy following mixing with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , which confirmed the existence of BTP at the surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film and the interaction of ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=O}$ groups with oxygen vacancy and/or uncoordinated $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ . Simultaneously, this suggests that a much stronger interaction happened between $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ and $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ as compared to $-\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ . Figure 1e presents the chemical shifts for $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ spectra of pure BTP and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -BTP when dissolved in DMSO-d6. The chemical shift of $\mathrm{~F~}$ was moved from $-74.06\mathrm{ppm}$ in pure BTF to $-74.61\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -BTP, again demonstrating that the ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ group can interact with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . To consolidate evidence of the interaction between $-\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ or $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=O}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , we conducted $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectra measurements for BTP and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -BTP in a DMSO-d6 solution (Figure S1a). All C atoms in $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{P}+\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ exhibited notable chemical shifts, with their signals shifting towards the low field. It has been observed in Figure $1\mathrm{f-h}$ that the peaks associated with $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ (position 4) and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ (position 3) experienced a greater shift in chemical shifts $(>0.3\mathrm{ppm})$ compared to the positions 1 and 7 of the BTP molecule corresponding to ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ (approximately $0.2\mathrm{ppm}$ . This certified that $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ groups interacted more strongly with $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ compared to ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ . Meantime, this also revealed that multiple groups can simultaneously interact with $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ , which effectively passivated oxygen vacancy and/ or uncoordinated $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ defects. As exhibited in Figure S1b, the $\mathrm{C-F}$ stretching vibration peak of pure BTP was observed at $1069\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , while the stretching vibration peak of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ - BTP was found at $1074\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .[36] This increase in wavenumber suggests an interaction between the ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ functional group and $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ . The stretching vibration peak of the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=O}$ bond in pure BTP was positioned at $1175\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which was shifted to a higher wavenumber $(1180\mathrm{cm}^{-1}),$ ) after interacting with $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ .[37,38] In short, the various experimental evidence collectively revealed the strong interaction of multiple groups in BTP with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , which is beneficial for fully passivating oxygen vacancy and/or uncoordinated Sn4+ defects. Combining ab-initio molecular dynamics simulations can help explain the adsorption and binding process of BTP on the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ surface. During the simulation process, $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ are more likely to form coordination bonds with uncoordinated Sn than -F (Figure 1i), which is consistent with the XPS and NMR results. The binding energy $(E_{\mathrm{{b}}})$ between the BTP modifier and the top surface of the ETL was calculated as $E_{\mathrm{{BTP/ETL}}}{-}E_{\mathrm{{ETL}}}{-}E_{\mathrm{{BTP}}}$ , where $E_{\mathrm{BTP/ETL}},E_{\mathrm{ETL}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{BTP}}$ represent the total energy of the adsorption system, ETL system, and BTP molecule, respectively. The $E_{\mathrm{{b}}}$ between BTP and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ was $-7.71\ \mathrm{eV}$ .
In the N 1s spectra (Figure S2a), the peak corresponding to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}{}^{+}$ underwent a shift from $400.61\mathrm{eV}$ for the FAI film to $400.48\mathrm{eV}$ for the FAI $+\mathbf{B}\mathrm{TP}$ film, which could be attributed to the formation of a hydrogen bond between $\mathrm{NH}_{3}{}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{~F~}$ or O. Similar results were also observed in perovskite films of different perovskite compositions (Figure S2e,f). Compared to the pure BTP film, the binding energies of F 1s, $\mathrm{~P~}2\mathrm{p}$ , and $^{\mathrm{~\small~O~}}$ 1s in $\mathbf{FAI+BTP}$ exhibited a shift towards higher values (Figure S2b–d). Specifically, the O 1s peak of pure BTP at $530.20\mathrm{eV}$ and the F 1s peak of pure BTP at $688.48\mathrm{eV}$ were shifted to $531.22\mathrm{eV}$ and $688.70\mathrm{eV}$ in $\mathrm{FAI+BTP}$ , respectively. The presence of hydrogen bonds, specifically $\mathrm{N-H\cdotsF}$ and $\mathrm{N-H\mathrm{...O}}$ bonds, plays a significant role in suppressing the formation of cation vacancy defects and inhibiting cation migration. Similarly, the molecular dynamics simulations also revealed that $-\mathrm{F}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=O}$ in BTP can form weak chemical interactions with FA cations, and the binding energy of BTP with $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ containing FA vacancy defect is $-0.213\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S3).
In addition, it is imperative to further investigate and confirm the chemical interaction between BTP and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . The molecular dynamics simulation was performed to explore the interaction between BTP and $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ containing iodine vacancy defects. From Figure 2a, it was found that $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ preferentially binds to uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . The binding energy between BTP and uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ on the surface of perovskite is $-3.89\mathrm{eV}$ . In Figure 2b, the Pb 4f spectra of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ exhibited two prominent peaks at 138.71 and $143.58\mathrm{eV}$ , which corresponded to Pb $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}$ $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ respectively. In contrast, the $\mathrm{Pb~4f}$ peaks for the $\mathbf{P}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{I}_{2}+\mathbf{B}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{P}$ film (138.37 and $143.23\mathrm{eV}$ ) exhibited a downward shift in binding energy. It can be observed in Figure 2c that the binding energies of $\mathrm{~I~}3\mathrm{d}$ for $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film were at 619.42 and $630.90\mathrm{eV}$ , which were shifted to 619.31 and $630.79\mathrm{eV}$ after incorporating BTP, respectively. The observed shifts in the Pb 4f and I 3d peaks provided evidence of the interaction between BTP and $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ . In comparison with pure BTP film, the F 1s, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~P~}}2\boldsymbol{\mathrm{p}}$ and O 1s peaks of $\mathbf{P}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{I}_{2}+\mathbf{B}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{P}$ film exhibited a noticeable shift towards higher binding energy, with an increase of $0.37\mathrm{eV}$ , $2.85\mathrm{eV}$ and $1.41\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively (Figure 2d–f). From the FTIR spectra in Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ , the ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ stretching vibration of pure BTP was located at $1069\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which was shifted to $1074\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ after mixing with perovskite, indicating that ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ interacted with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and/or $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ in perovskite. The stretching vibration peak of the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=O}$ bond in pure BTP was observed at $1175\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which was shifted to $1170\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in ${\bf B T P+}$ perovskite, confirming the interaction of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=O}$ with undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . In addition, the stretching vibration peak of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ was shifted from $1748\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of BTP to $1743\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of BTP + perovskite, which is suggestive of the interaction between $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C=O}$ and undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . Like $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ ETL, the multiple groups in BTP can simultaneously with perovskites via coordination bond and/or hydrogen bond. It can be concluded that BTP can form strong chelating interaction with both the perovskite layer and ETL by multiple active sites. When we simultaneously establish $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ and perovskite structures for calculation (Figure 2h), we found that the F atoms on both sides can form chemical bonds with uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and uncoordinated $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ , which can bridge $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ . This further confirmed that we have successfully developed multi-dentate chelating molecules to effectively bridge $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and perovskite layer and to strengthen interfacial contact.
To study the electrical performance of the electronic transport layer before and after BTP modification, we conducted the current–voltage curves $(I{-}V)$ measurements. In Figure S4, it can be easily observed that the $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}/\mathbf{B}\mathbf{TP}$ film demonstrated higher conductivity in comparison with the bare $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film, which is ascribed to reduced oxygen vacancy and/or uncoordinated $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ defects due to the effective passivation of BTP. A similar surface roughness was found in the ETL without and with BTP, as shown in Figure 3a,b. After BTP modification, the water contact angle of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film was increased (Figure S5), which should be beneficial for suppressing heterogeneous nucleation and promoting homogeneous nucleation of perovskite, which resulted in generating highaspectratio perovskite grains. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were collected to evaluate the influence of interface modulation on the crystallinity of the perovskite films (Figure 3c and d). Compared to the control film, the modified perovskite films showed slightly increased diffraction peak intensity and reduced peak width at half height (FWHM), indicating enhanced crystallinity and preferred orientation. This implies that BTP interface modification can promote perovskite crystallization. We conducted grazing incident X-ray diffraction (GIXRD) measurements with different grazing incident angles ω (ranging from $0.5^{\circ}$ to $1.5^{\circ}{}^{\cdot}$ ) to evaluate the degree of residual tensile strain. Because of the high diffraction angle and the multiplicative factor, the (211) plane diffraction peak was chosen for further analysis due to its more accurate structure symmetry information.[39] It can be observed that the BTP-modified perovskite film had less tensile strain compared to the control film (Figure 3e and f), which should be put down to reduced interfacial defects and improved perovskite crystallization.
The surface morphologies of control and BTP-modified perovskite films were examined using top-view scanning electron microscopy (SEM), as shown in Figure 4a, b and Figure S6. The control film had an inhomogeneous grain structure and large particles of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ distributed at grain boundaries. Moreover, the adjacent grains are not dense. However, the modified perovskite film possessed a much more compact and uniform grain. The average grain size was enlarged from $691.7{\mathrm{nm}}$ to $1027.6\mathrm{nm}$ after polydentate ligand chelating (Figure S6a–d). Meanwhile, the larger grain size can be also observed from the cross-sectional SEM images (Figure S6e and f). The improved morphology, increased grain size and crystallinity can contribute to ameliorated carrier transport and suppressed nonradiative recombination. Similar results were observed in the AFM images, and the perovskite film modified with BTP showed decreased roughness values and fewer grain boundaries (Figure 4c and d). The improved film surface morphology would provide better film quality and interfacial contact with the hole transport layer (HTL).[25,40,41] The BTP buried interface modification resulted in an increased contact angle of perovskite films, which is attributed to enhanced crystallinity and enlarged grain size (Figure S7).
Subsequently, we gained insights into the effect of polydentate ligand interface modification on the carrier recombination of perovskite films. Obviously, the photoluminescence (PL) intensity was much enhanced following BTP modification (Figure $\mathsf{4e{\mathrm{-g}}},$ ). At the same time, the BTP modification led to a much-increased carrier lifetime from $2.5~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ to $4.8~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ (Figure 4h and Table S1). The increased PL intensity and carrier lifetime is associated with reduced nonradiative recombination because of effective defect passivation and improved crystallization. To investigate the defect densities of the perovskite films without and with BTP modification, we analyzed the space-charge limited currents (SCLC) by measuring dark current–voltage $(I{-}V)$ curves. The voltage at the kink point between the ohmic region and the trap-filling region in the dark $I{-}V$ curve is defined as the trap-filled limit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ . The following equation was used to calculate trap density:[42]
$$
n_{\mathrm{t}}=(2\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}V_{\mathrm{TFL}})/(e L^{2})
$$
where $\varepsilon_{0},\varepsilon,e$ and $L$ are the vacuum permittivity, the relative dielectric constant of perovskite, the elementary charge, and the thickness of the perovskite film, respectively. After modification, the trap density was distinctly decreased from $5.33{\times}10^{15}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ to $3.55{\times}10^{15}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ (Figure 4i and j). The effective passivation of defects from the top surface of ETL and bottom surface of perovskite films should be liable for reduced trap density and thereby inhibited trap-induced nonradiative recombination, which contributed to stabilizing the buried interface.
The charge transfer and extraction process were further investigated by PL and TRPL measurements. After buried interface manipulation, the PL intensity was reduced and carrier lifetime was shortened from 255.25 ns to 152.07 ns (Figure 4k, l and Table S1), which indicates the facilitated electron extraction due to reduced interfacial defects and more favorable energy band alignment. The transient photocurrent (TPC) test was utilized to further study the charge extraction and transfer of the devices with and without BTP. As can be seen in Figure 5a, the BTP-modified device $(1.90\upmu\mathrm{s})$ showed a shorter carrier lifetime compared with the control device $(2.75\upmu\mathrm{s})$ , proving promoted carrier extraction and transfer, which is in good agreement with TRPL results. To evaluate the carrier recombination dynamics of devices, the transient photovoltage (TPV) curves of PSCs were measured (Figure 5b). The device modified with BTP indicated a remarkably slower decay compared to the control device. The charge carrier decay lifetimes were $3.5~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ for the device with BTP, which was much longer than $0.97\upmu\mathrm{s}$ for the control device. The longer decay lifetime indicates that the devices with BTP modifier much suppressed interfacial trap-assisted nonradiative recombination, which is conducive to strengthening interfacial stability. Figure 5c illustrates the relationship between open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ and light intensity $(I)$ for the control and modified devices. The ideality factor $(m)$ is defined as the slope of n $k_{\mathrm{B}}T/q$ of the light intensity-dependent $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ , where $k_{\mathrm{B}}$ , $T$ , and $q$ represent Boltzmann’s constant, Kelvin temperature, and elementary charge, respectively. The slope of $1k_{B}T/q$ indicates a trap-free condition, while the $m$ over 1 suggests the presence of recombination induced by defects. The $m$ value was reduced from 1.83 for the control device to 1.31 for the BTP device, confirming that the surface trapassisted nonradiative recombination was significantly reduced.
UV photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) was employed to study the energy levels of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with or without BTP. The band gap $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ of $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ $(4.02\mathrm{eV})$ was not affected by surface modification with BTP (Figure S8). As shown in Figure 5d and Figure S9, the work function (WF) was reduced from $-5.15\mathrm{eV}$ for the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film to $-4.84\mathrm{eV}$ for the BTP-modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layers. The decrease in WF is attributed to the formation of an interface dipole caused by the bending of the vacuum level. Furthermore, the calculated valence band maximum $(E_{\mathrm{VB}})$ values were $-8.69\mathrm{eV}$ for the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $-8.35\mathrm{eV}$ for the $\mathrm{BTF}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . After BTP modification, the conduction band minimum $(E_{\mathrm{CB}})$ of $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ was changed from $-4.67$ to $-4.33\mathrm{eV}$ . The $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ of the perovskite films without and with BTP was not changed (Figure S10). The interfacial energy barrier was reduced from $0.36\mathrm{eV}$ to $0.02\mathrm{eV}$ , which can promote electron extraction and transfer.
The modification mechanisms of the polydentate ligand BTP for the buried interface are illustrated in Figure 5e. On the one hand, strong chemical interaction will be formed between F, $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ in BTP and $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , which passivated the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ and/or oxygen vacancy defects. On the other hand, these functional groups also can coordinate with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , which healed undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and/or halide vacancy defects. The hydrogen bonds will be formed between $-\mathrm{F}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ in BTP and ${\bf-N H}_{2}{\bf-C}{\bf=N H}_{3}{\bf\Sigma}^{+}$ , which is favorable for suppressing the formation of $\mathrm{FA}^{+}$ vacancy and cation migration. In conclusion, the perovskite layer and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer were chemically linked by the synergistic effect of multiple groups in BTP, which heightened the stability of the buried interface.
In order to study if $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ , ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=O}$ groups have a synergistic effect, 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol (TFE), methyl acetate (MAC), and phosphoric acid (PA) were employed as reference molecules. The effect of these molecules on photovoltaic performance was compared systematically (Figure S11–S14 and Table S2–S5). Obviously, all modification molecules exhibited a positive effect in enhancing device performance while the BTP was the most effective (Figure 6a–d). This signifies that $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ , ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ groups had an excellent synergistic effect, which highlights the importance of the synergistic passivation of multiple groups in BTP. The control device exhibited an optimal PCE of $21.94\%$ with a short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of $24.26\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.132\mathrm{V}$ , and a fill factor (FF) of 0.799. In contrast, the best-performing BTP-modified device achieved an attractive PCE of $24.26\%$ (with a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $25.17\mathrm{A}/$ $\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.170\mathrm{V}$ , and FF of 0.824). The hysteresis factor (HI) was reduced from $3.56\%$ of the control device to $0.37\%$ of the BTP-modified device, which is due to reduced interfacial defects, effectively suppressed the migration of halogen vacancies and $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ vacancies, improved film quality, reduced the charge barrier, reduced interface electron accumulation and facilitated interfacial electron extraction.[35,43] To demonstrate the universality of our polydentate ligand chelating strategy, the PSCs based on $\mathrm{FA}_{0.85}\mathrm{MA}_{0.15}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ without and with BTP manipulation were also fabricated (Figure 6e and Table 1). The BTP modification resulted in an increase in PCE from $22.84\%$ $(J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $24.79\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.160\mathrm{V}$ , and FF of 0.794) to $24.63\%$ $\ensuremath{\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{sc}}}$ of $25.04~\mathrm{A/cm}^{2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.195\mathrm{V}$ , and FF of 0.823). This attests to the universality of our multidentate ligand chelating strategy. The incident photo-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra of the devices without and with BTP and the corresponding integrated current density are depicted in
Figure S15. After BTP modification, the improvement in IPCE intensity mainly stemmed from optimized energy band alignment and promoted electron extraction. The calculated integrated current densities of the control and BTP-passivated devices were 24.04 and $24.77\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , respectively, which is in good accordance with $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ from $J_{-}V$ measurements. The steady-state output current density and PCE for all PSCs at the maximum power point under one sun illumination for 400 seconds are exhibited in Figure 6f and Table 2. The BTP-modified device delivered a stable PCE of $23.49\%$ , which is much higher than $21.11\%$ . In short, the significant PCE enhancement was enabled by the polydentate ligand chelating medication for the buried interface.
Since multidentate ligand BTP can chemically link the perovskite layer and $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ layer, it should be able to strengthen the stability of the buried interface. Therefore, we investigated the long-term stability of the unencapsulated control and modified devices. After heating at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere for $1728\mathrm{h}$ , the control device suffered from $74.4\%$ efficiency loss whereas the BTP-modified device retained $84.2\%$ of its initial PCE (Figure 6g and Figure S16). It was evident that the PSCs with the BTP modifier exhibited better thermal stability than the control device. The humidity stability test of the unsealed devices was conducted in the ambient environment with an RH of $10-$ $20\%$ (Figure 6h and Figure S17). After aging for $3120\mathrm{h}$ , the BTP-treated device retained $98.6\%$ of its original efficiency whereas the control device preserved $82.3\%$ of its initial PCE value. The improved moisture stability mainly resulted from reduced grain boundaries and improved crystallization. Furthermore, the light stability of the control and BTPmodified devices was further evaluated under one sun illumination of $100\mathrm{mW}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ provided by white light LED in an ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere (Figure 6i and Figure S18). Compared to the control device with $23.7\%$ retention of original PCE after $1728\mathrm{h}$ , the device with BTP showed a substantial improvement in light stability, maintaining $54.5\%$ of the initial PCE. The reduced interfacial trap density and heightened buried interface contact should be mainly responsible for increased stability under light and heat stress. In a word, the polydentate ligand chelating reinforced buried interface stability should be the main reason for ameliorated long-term device stability.
## Conclusion
In summary, we developed a polydentate ligand reinforced chelating strategy to stabilize buried interface by passivating interfacial defects and releasing interfacial stress. It was revealed that the multiple functional groups in BTP, including $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ and two $-\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ , can synergistically passivate the defects from the surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (uncoordinated $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ and oxygen vacancy) and from the bottom surface of perovskite layer (e.g., undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , halide vacancy defects). At the same time, the interfacial residual tensile stress was mitigated, perovskite crystallization was promoted, and the interfacial energy barrier was reduced. The BTP manipulation enabled much reduced nonradiative recombination and heightened buried interface contact. The multidentate ligand modulation strategy is universal for different perovskite compositions. The PCE of as high as $24.63\%$ was achieved following BTP modification, which is among the highest efficiencies ever reported for the PSCs fabricated in ambient conditions. The BTP-modified unencapsulated devices maintained $98.6\%$ of their initial PCE after over 3000 hours of aging in the ambient environment under a relative humidity (RH) of $10{-}20\%$ , and $84.2\%$ of their original PCE after $1728\mathrm{h}$ of thermal stress at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ This work provides insights into strengthening the stability of the buried interface by engineering multidentate chelating ligand molecules.
